\section*{Abstract}

A core skill in empirical science is to separate pattern from noise: judging whether an apparent signal in the data reflects a reproducible process or the combined effects of variability, measurement limitations, sampling design, and model assumptions. To make such judgements more reliable, researchers typically follow a workflow—from posing a question and formulating hypotheses and predictions, to designing a study, collecting data, fitting statistical models, and interpreting and communicating results. Yet repeated methodological critiques across disciplines have highlighted persistent weaknesses in this scientific workflow, including vague or weakly testable hypotheses, insufficient sample sizes, and an overreliance on null-hypothesis significance testing that promotes dichotomous “significant / non-significant” interpretations and selective reporting.\\
Here we propose a small but practical addition to the workflow: a “simulation loop” implemented before data collection (or early in analysis) to make assumptions explicit and to anticipate what the planned data can and cannot reveal. The loop has three steps: (1) translate a conceptual hypothesis into an explicit generative (mechanistic) model; (2) simulate and visualise data implied by that model to scrutinise assumptions about effect sizes, variability, and measurement limitations; and (3) iterate design choices (e.g. sample size, treatment levels, the range of predictor values, measurement error, model structure) using criteria focused on estimation quality—especially interval widths (precision), bias, and robustness to plausible misspecification—rather than binary detect/not-detect framing.\\ 
We provide worked examples with reproducible R code, including linear and non-linear cases, and show how the simulation loop can reveal identifiability limits, guide sampling toward decision-relevant precision, and increase transparency in modelling choices.

We argue that these benefits become increasingly important as ecological analyses draw on larger datasets and AI-assisted pattern discovery, where deciding what to believe depends on clearly stated assumptions and defensible uncertainty quantification.\\



%Science continually seeks to explain patterns in nature that are often obscured by the noise of variation caused by a myriad of influencing factors. To disentangle pattern from noise and build upon the existing body of knowledge, researchers adhere to a scientific workflow. However, the replication crisis has highlighted serious problems about many aspects of the common scientific procedure: the formulation of meaningless (null) hypotheses, insufficient sample size, the problematic usage and interpretation of p-values that led to biased reportings and distortion of the literature. \\
%Here, we propose the integration of data simulation into the scientific workflow to address these challenges by: 1) developing meaningful and testable hypotheses through the mathematical formulation of mechanistic models, 2) manipulating effect size, variance and sample size to generate synthetic/fake datasets, 3) exploring the potential and limitations of both the underlying model and the data obtained, and 4) drawing conclusions that can reliably build upon the existing `body of knowledge'.\\
%We expect that in a future dominated by powerful AI and machine learning algorithms applied to ever-larger datasets, meaningful, theoretically grounded hypotheses will be more important than ever for revealing underlying causalities. Without the understanding of causality, we may excel at making predictions within known limits, yet fail to understand the driving variables behind them.\\

\textbf{Keywords}: Bayesian statistics, statistcal methods, quantitative ecology, hypothesis testing, scientific reasoning, experimental design, power analysis
