\section*{3 Practical example (linear): Plant growth and nitrogen}

Data simulation is best shown in practice. The example presented here is deliberately simple and can be reproduced directly. A fully commented R script containing all code shown below (including the simulation loops and figures) can be downloaded from the Supplement.

\lstset{
	language=R,
	basicstyle=\ttfamily\small,
	commentstyle=\ttfamily\small,
	keywordstyle=\ttfamily\small,
	stringstyle=\ttfamily\small,
	showstringspaces=false,
	breaklines=true,
	frame=single,
	framerule=0.4pt
}

\subsection*{What influences $y$ in what way?}

Every study starts with a research question. Here: What is the influence of nitrogen fertilization on plant growth? We might perform an experiment on sunflowers and fertilize some with nitrogen to assess biomass after one season. Our response variable $y$ is biomass, and a verbal hypothesis could be that plants grow more when nitrogen increases.

To translate this hypothesis into a mechanistic (mathematical) model, we assume a positive linear relationship between biomass and nitrogen concentration $x$. Here, the ``mechanistic'' component is intentionally simple and phenomenological; later examples extend the same workflow to explicitly non-linear response functions. A simple model is:
\begin{equation}
	y_i = a + b x_i + \varepsilon_i, \qquad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma)
\end{equation}
where $a$ is the intercept (expected biomass at $x=0$ on the chosen scale), $b$ is the slope (expected biomass increase per unit nitrogen; an effect size), and $\sigma$ captures variability around the mean relationship (process variation and/or measurement error).

\subsection*{Setting model parameters}

This is where things get interesting and concrete: What do we think these model parameters should look like? In other words, what biomass do we expect at low nitrogen, how strong is the nitrogen effect, and how variable is the relationship?

For illustration, we choose:
\begin{lstlisting}
	# Specify model parameters
	a <- 30     # intercept
	b <- 7      # slope (effect size)
	sigma <- 5  # SD of residual variation
\end{lstlisting}

These values are not ``truth''; they make our assumptions explicit. Later, we can explore how conclusions change when we vary them.

\subsection*{Simulate!}

To simulate, we specify a sample size and generate a plausible range of nitrogen concentrations. Here we treat nitrogen as a continuous gradient:
\begin{lstlisting}
	# Choose a design and simulate data
	n <- 50
	x <- rnorm(n, mean = 10, sd = 4)         # nitrogen concentrations
	y <- a + b*x + rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = sigma)
	
	fake <- data.frame(x = x, y = y)
\end{lstlisting}

In experiments with fixed treatment levels (rather than a gradient), $x$ would be set to those levels with replication per level, rather than drawn from a distribution. The same simulation logic applies; only the design for generating $x$ changes.

\subsection*{Visualize and assess your fake data}

Now we plot the generated data and check whether it matches what we had in mind. This is the hypothesis in visual form, including both effect size and variability.
\begin{lstlisting}
	plot(fake$x, fake$y,
	xlab = "Nitrogen concentration (x)",
	ylab = "Biomass (y)",
	main = "Simulated (fake) data")
\end{lstlisting}

We can also fit the planned model and check whether the estimated parameters are in the range we assumed. This is a quick sanity check that the analysis can recover the generating values under these assumptions.
\begin{lstlisting}
	fit_lm <- lm(y ~ x, data = fake)
	summary(fit_lm)
	confint(fit_lm)  # 95% confidence intervals
	
	plot(fake$x, fake$y,
	xlab = "Nitrogen concentration (x)",
	ylab = "Biomass (y)",
	main = "Simulated data with fitted regression line")
	abline(fit_lm)
\end{lstlisting}

At this point, it is tempting to focus on a p-value. In the simulation loop, we instead look at the estimates and their uncertainty---for example, the width of the 95\% confidence interval for $b$.

\subsection*{How to use this --- Play!}

Now we go back and forth and explore what changes when we manipulate assumptions and design choices. A simple and practical target is precision: how wide are uncertainty intervals for the parameter we care about (here the nitrogen effect $b$), and how does that depend on sample size, the range of nitrogen values, or the amount of variability and measurement error?

The code below repeats the simulated study many times and summarises the width of the 95\% confidence interval for $b$ across repetitions. These design--precision curves are conditional on the assumed model (linearity, error distribution, and constant variance); later examples show how to relax these assumptions and test sensitivity.
\begin{lstlisting}
	# One simulation run: generate data, fit model, return CI width for slope b
	one_run <- function(n, a, b, sigma, mean_x = 10, sd_x = 4) {
		x <- rnorm(n, mean = mean_x, sd = sd_x)
		y <- a + b*x + rnorm(n, 0, sigma)
		fit <- lm(y ~ x)
		ci <- confint(fit)["x", ]
		return(ci[2] - ci[1])
	}
	
	set.seed(1)
	
	# Precision vs sample size
	ns <- c(10, 20, 30, 50, 80, 120)
	R <- 500
	
	out_n <- data.frame(n = ns, mean_width = NA, sd_width = NA)
	for (j in seq_along(ns)) {
		widths <- replicate(R, one_run(ns[j], a, b, sigma))
		out_n$mean_width[j] <- mean(widths)
		out_n$sd_width[j] <- sd(widths)
	}
	
	plot(out_n$n, out_n$mean_width, type = "b",
	xlab = "Sample size (n)",
	ylab = "Mean 95% CI width for slope (b)",
	main = "Precision of the nitrogen effect vs sample size")
\end{lstlisting}

If a study requires the nitrogen effect to be estimated within $\pm k$ biomass units per nitrogen unit, then a rough design target is a 95\% confidence interval width of about $2k$ for $b$. The simulation loop provides a direct way to choose $n$ (and other design choices) to meet such a target under stated assumptions.

A second lever in regression is the range of the predictor: even with the same $n$, a narrow nitrogen range makes the slope harder to estimate. We can explore that directly:
\begin{lstlisting}
	# Precision vs predictor range (sd of x), at fixed n
	sd_x_values <- c(1, 2, 4, 6)
	n_fixed <- 50
	
	out_x <- data.frame(sd_x = sd_x_values, mean_width = NA)
	for (k in seq_along(sd_x_values)) {
		widths <- replicate(R, one_run(n_fixed, a, b, sigma,
		mean_x = 10, sd_x = sd_x_values[k]))
		out_x$mean_width[k] <- mean(widths)
	}
	
	plot(out_x$sd_x, out_x$mean_width, type = "b",
	xlab = "SD of nitrogen values (predictor range)",
	ylab = "Mean 95% CI width for slope (b)",
	main = "Precision depends on predictor range")
\end{lstlisting}

Finally, we can explore how increased variability and measurement error affect precision. For example, if the residual standard deviation doubles, uncertainty intervals widen substantially under the same design:
\begin{lstlisting}
	# Sensitivity to variability / measurement error: larger sigma
	sigma2 <- 10
	
	out_n2 <- data.frame(n = ns, mean_width = NA)
	for (j in seq_along(ns)) {
		widths <- replicate(R, one_run(ns[j], a, b, sigma2))
		out_n2$mean_width[j] <- mean(widths)
	}
	
	plot(out_n$n, out_n$mean_width, type = "b",
	xlab = "Sample size (n)",
	ylab = "Mean 95% CI width for slope (b)",
	main = "Precision vs sample size under different variability")
	lines(out_n2$n, out_n2$mean_width, type = "b", lty = 2)
	legend("topright", legend = c(paste0("sigma = ", sigma),
	paste0("sigma = ", sigma2)),
	lty = c(1, 2), bty = "n")
\end{lstlisting}

This back-and-forth is the core of the simulation loop in practice: we make assumptions explicit, generate the data those assumptions imply, and then explore how design choices translate into the uncertainty of our key estimates. Improving precision, however, does not by itself make an association causal; the loop complements, but does not replace, careful causal reasoning and design. In the next section, we extend the same workflow to non-linear relationships, where model structure and identifiability become even more central.


\section*{4 Practical example (non-linear): Saturating growth response to nitrogen}

Many ecological relationships are non-linear: responses can saturate, show thresholds, or peak at intermediate conditions. In such cases, simulation is especially useful because the same data can often be explained by different parameter combinations, and identifiability can depend strongly on the predictor range and the design. Below we extend the same workflow to a simple saturating response of biomass to nitrogen, using a Michaelis--Menten (rectangular hyperbola) function.

\subsection*{What influences $y$ in what way?}

We keep the same research question---how nitrogen influences plant growth---but now assume that growth increases with nitrogen and then levels off as other resources become limiting. A simple non-linear model is:
\begin{equation}
	y_i = \frac{V_{\max} x_i}{K + x_i} + \varepsilon_i, \qquad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma)
\end{equation}
where $V_{\max}$ is the asymptotic biomass (maximum expected response as $x \to \infty$), $K$ is the half-saturation constant (the nitrogen value at which expected biomass is $V_{\max}/2$), and $\sigma$ captures variability and measurement error around the mean response.

\subsection*{Setting model parameters}

As before, we must state plausible parameter values. For illustration, we choose:
\begin{lstlisting}
	# Non-linear model parameters (Michaelis-Menten)
	Vmax <- 120   # asymptotic biomass
	K <- 8        # half-saturation constant
	sigma <- 10   # residual SD
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection*{Simulate!}

A key design choice in non-linear models is the range of $x$. To show why this matters, we simulate two designs with the same sample size: one that samples nitrogen only in a low-to-moderate range (where saturation is not well observed), and one that samples a wider range that includes values high enough to approach the asymptote.

\begin{lstlisting}
	# Helper: Michaelis-Menten mean function
	mu_mm <- function(x, Vmax, K) Vmax * x / (K + x)
	
	set.seed(2)
	n <- 50
	
	# Design A: narrow nitrogen range (poor leverage on asymptote)
	x_narrow <- runif(n, min = 0, max = 12)
	
	# Design B: wide nitrogen range (better leverage on asymptote)
	x_wide <- runif(n, min = 0, max = 40)
	
	# Simulate data
	y_narrow <- mu_mm(x_narrow, Vmax, K) + rnorm(n, 0, sigma)
	y_wide   <- mu_mm(x_wide,   Vmax, K) + rnorm(n, 0, sigma)
	
	fake_narrow <- data.frame(x = x_narrow, y = y_narrow)
	fake_wide   <- data.frame(x = x_wide,   y = y_wide)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection*{Visualize and assess your fake data}

Plotting the two simulated datasets immediately shows why the design matters: in the narrow range, the relationship can look almost linear, whereas the wide range reveals curvature and the onset of saturation.

\begin{lstlisting}
	plot(fake_narrow$x, fake_narrow$y,
	xlab = "Nitrogen (x)", ylab = "Biomass (y)",
	main = "Non-linear model: narrow nitrogen range")
	curve(mu_mm(x, Vmax, K), add = TRUE)
	
	plot(fake_wide$x, fake_wide$y,
	xlab = "Nitrogen (x)", ylab = "Biomass (y)",
	main = "Non-linear model: wide nitrogen range")
	curve(mu_mm(x, Vmax, K), add = TRUE)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection*{How to use this --- Play!}

We now fit the planned non-linear model and examine what it typically produces under each design. In base R, a simple option is \texttt{nls} (non-linear least squares). Non-linear fitting can be sensitive to starting values, so we provide reasonable starts.

\begin{lstlisting}
	# Fit non-linear model with nls
	fit_mm <- function(dat) {
		nls(y ~ Vmax * x / (K + x),
		data = dat,
		start = list(Vmax = max(dat$y), K = median(dat$x)),
		control = nls.control(maxiter = 200, warnOnly = TRUE))
	}
	
	fitA <- fit_mm(fake_narrow)
	fitB <- fit_mm(fake_wide)
	
	summary(fitA)
	summary(fitB)
\end{lstlisting}

Even when \texttt{nls} converges, the key question is not whether parameters exist, but how uncertain they are and how much they depend on design choices. A simple, comparable metric is again interval width. For \texttt{nls}, we can approximate confidence intervals for parameters. (In some datasets, \texttt{confint} may fail or be slow; in that case, repeating the simulation and examining the spread of estimates provides the same message.)

\begin{lstlisting}
	# Approximate 95% confidence intervals for parameters (may be slow for some fits)
	confint(fitA)
	confint(fitB)
\end{lstlisting}

To make the comparison more robust and aligned with the simulation loop, we repeat the whole study many times under each design and summarise the distribution of parameter estimates. This makes identifiability issues visible: if many different (Vmax, K) combinations fit the data similarly, the estimates will vary widely across repetitions, especially under the narrow-range design.

\begin{lstlisting}
	one_run_mm <- function(n, x_max, Vmax, K, sigma) {
		x <- runif(n, 0, x_max)
		y <- mu_mm(x, Vmax, K) + rnorm(n, 0, sigma)
		dat <- data.frame(x = x, y = y)
		
		fit <- try(fit_mm(dat), silent = TRUE)
		if (inherits(fit, "try-error")) return(c(Vmax_hat = NA, K_hat = NA))
		
		co <- coef(fit)
		c(Vmax_hat = unname(co["Vmax"]), K_hat = unname(co["K"]))
	}
	
	set.seed(3)
	R <- 300
	n <- 50
	
	# Design A: x_max = 12 (narrow); Design B: x_max = 40 (wide)
	estA <- replicate(R, one_run_mm(n, x_max = 12, Vmax, K, sigma))
	estB <- replicate(R, one_run_mm(n, x_max = 40, Vmax, K, sigma))
	
	estA <- t(estA); estB <- t(estB)
	estA <- as.data.frame(estA); estB <- as.data.frame(estB)
	
	# Remove failed fits
	estA <- estA[complete.cases(estA), ]
	estB <- estB[complete.cases(estB), ]
	
	# Compare distributions of estimates
	summary(estA)
	summary(estB)
	
	plot(estA$K_hat, estA$Vmax_hat,
	xlab = "K estimate", ylab = "Vmax estimate",
	main = "Narrow range: strong parameter trade-off")
	abline(v = K, h = Vmax, lty = 2)
	
	plot(estB$K_hat, estB$Vmax_hat,
	xlab = "K estimate", ylab = "Vmax estimate",
	main = "Wide range: improved identifiability")
	abline(v = K, h = Vmax, lty = 2)
\end{lstlisting}

This ``cloud of estimates'' is a simple diagnostic: in the narrow-range design, $V_{\max}$ and $K$ often trade off against each other (many combinations produce similar curves over $x \in [0,12]$), so parameter uncertainty can remain large even with the same sample size. In contrast, including higher nitrogen values provides leverage to distinguish curvature and the asymptote, improving identifiability and reducing uncertainty.

Finally, we can connect this directly back to design planning by comparing a precision proxy (e.g.\ the interquartile range of estimates or the spread of fitted parameters) across choices of $n$ and $x$-range. For example:

\begin{lstlisting}
	# Quick design exploration: how does n affect the spread of K estimates?
	ns <- c(20, 50, 100)
	R <- 200
	
	spread <- function(n, x_max) {
		est <- replicate(R, one_run_mm(n, x_max, Vmax, K, sigma))
		est <- t(est)
		est <- est[complete.cases(est), , drop = FALSE]
		# use IQR as a robust spread measure
		c(IQR_Vmax = IQR(est[, "Vmax_hat"]), IQR_K = IQR(est[, "K_hat"]))
	}
	
	out <- expand.grid(n = ns, x_max = c(12, 40))
	out$IQR_Vmax <- NA
	out$IQR_K <- NA
	
	for (i in seq_len(nrow(out))) {
		s <- spread(out$n[i], out$x_max[i])
		out$IQR_Vmax[i] <- s["IQR_Vmax"]
		out$IQR_K[i] <- s["IQR_K"]
	}
	
	out
	
	plot(out$n[out$x_max == 12], out$IQR_K[out$x_max == 12], type = "b",
	xlab = "Sample size (n)", ylab = "IQR of K estimates",
	main = "Precision depends on n and x-range")
	lines(out$n[out$x_max == 40], out$IQR_K[out$x_max == 40], type = "b", lty = 2)
	legend("topright", legend = c("x in [0,12]", "x in [0,40]"),
	lty = c(1,2), bty = "n")
\end{lstlisting}

This non-linear example illustrates why simulation is especially valuable beyond linear settings: identifiability and precision can depend as much on \emph{where} you sample (predictor range and design) as on how many observations you collect. In the next section, we generalise these ideas and briefly discuss how simulation-based thinking becomes even more important when models grow more complex (e.g.\ multiple predictors, interactions, hierarchical designs) and when inference relies on additional modelling assumptions.

\section*{5 Practical guidance: implementing the simulation loop}

This section summarises practical guidance to implement the simulation loop in everyday ecological research. The goal is not to add another layer of complexity, but to make assumptions explicit, align study design with the parameters we care about, and report simulation results transparently and reproducibly.

\subsection*{5.1 Practical guidance (short)}

\textbf{Choose a generative model that matches the question.}
Start from the verbal hypothesis and translate it into a simple generative model: define $y$, the predictors/design variables $x$, the expected response $f(x;\theta)$, and the stochastic component $\varepsilon$ (process variation and/or measurement error). Begin simple and add complexity only when it changes the inference you want to make (e.g.\ hierarchy, autocorrelation, heterogeneity, detection).

\textbf{State plausible parameter values (and ranges).}
Simulation is only as informative as the assumptions it explores. Use prior knowledge, the literature, pilot data, or clearly stated expert judgement to propose plausible values for effect sizes, variance components, and measurement limitations. When unsure, explore a range of values and report how conclusions depend on them.

\textbf{Simulate from the design you actually plan to run.}
Explicitly represent the sampling design (treatment levels, replication structure, predictor range, blocking, repeated measures). In non-linear settings, pay special attention to the predictor range: identifiability can depend as much on \emph{where} you sample as on how many observations you collect.

\textbf{Focus Step 3 on estimation, not thresholds.}
Treat Step 3 as ``playing through'' the planned study many times on the computer. Fit the planned analysis model and summarise what you typically get in terms of parameter estimates and uncertainty---especially uncertainty-interval widths (precision). Use bias and sensitivity to realistic departures from assumptions (e.g.\ increased noise, mild misspecification, measurement error) as additional diagnostics. This does not guarantee correct inference; it makes explicit what follows from the assumptions you are willing to make, and how sensitive results are to them.

\textbf{Decide design targets in terms of precision and relevance.}
If the scientific or management claim requires an effect to be estimated within $\pm k$ units on the chosen scale, plan for an interval width of about $2k$ for the relevant parameter. Use simulation outputs (design--precision curves) to choose $n$, predictor range, and measurement effort that meet this target under plausible assumptions.

\textbf{Report what you simulated and why.}
To make the workflow useful (and teachable), report the generative model, key assumptions, the range of parameters explored, the performance criterion (e.g.\ interval width), and the code required to reproduce results.

\begin{tcolorbox}[
	title=Checklist: reportable outputs of the simulation loop,
	colback=black!2,
	colframe=black!35,
	fonttitle=\bfseries,
	boxrule=0.6pt,
	arc=2mm,
	left=2mm,right=2mm,top=1mm,bottom=1mm
	]
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \textbf{Verbal hypothesis $\rightarrow$ model:} equations for $f(x;\theta)$ and the stochastic component ($\varepsilon$), including the response scale.
		\item \textbf{Assumptions:} plausible parameter values (and ranges) with justification (literature, pilot data, expert judgement).
		\item \textbf{Stochastic structure:} what $\varepsilon$ represents (process variation, measurement error, both), and whether hierarchy/autocorrelation is included.
		\item \textbf{Design explored:} $n$, treatment levels, predictor range, replication structure, measurement effort; what was varied and why.
		\item \textbf{Performance target:} interval width (precision) for key parameters; optionally bias and sensitivity to departures from assumptions.
		\item \textbf{Summary outputs:} design--precision curve(s) (e.g.\ interval width vs $n$, vs predictor range, vs noise).
		\item \textbf{Reproducibility:} code (and seed) to reproduce simulations and figures (e.g.\ in Supplement), plus software versions if relevant.
	\end{itemize}
\end{tcolorbox}

\subsection*{5.2 Common extensions (brief)}

The simulation loop is not restricted to linear regression. Common extensions follow the same three steps but use a different generative model and analysis model:

\textbf{Two-group designs.} A two-level treatment factor is a special case: simulate group means and variability, then examine the precision of the treatment contrast (difference in means) as a function of sample size and measurement error.

\textbf{Generalised responses.} For counts, proportions, presence/absence, and rates, specify an appropriate observation model (e.g.\ Poisson/negative binomial, binomial) and simulate on the natural scale. Precision targets may be set on the link scale or transformed back to the response scale.

\textbf{Hierarchical and repeated-measures designs.} Simulate nested structure (e.g.\ plots within sites, repeated measurements through time) and fit models with matching random effects or correlation structure. Examine how effective sample size and precision depend on the number of groups and replicates per group.

\textbf{Non-linear and mechanistic models.} When responses saturate, show thresholds, or depend on interacting processes, simulate under plausible parameter ranges and ensure the design includes predictor values that make key parameters identifiable (as illustrated in the non-linear example above).

\textbf{Model checking after fitting.} Simulation can also be used after model fitting (e.g.\ posterior predictive checks). In this paper we emphasise simulation earlier, when it can still inform study design and the formulation of quantitative hypotheses.


\section*{6 Outlook: why the simulation loop matters (especially with AI)}

This paper offers a compact way to make simulation part of routine ecological inference: turn a verbal hypothesis into an explicit generative model, simulate what it implies, and iterate design and modelling choices based on the estimates you would obtain and their uncertainty, rather than on whether a test crosses an arbitrary threshold.

A practical motivation is that different analysis choices sometimes lead to similar conclusions, but sometimes they do not. When results are fragile to reasonable alternatives (e.g.\ link functions, distributional assumptions, treatment of non-independence, or measurement error), it becomes hard to know what to believe. The simulation loop provides a constructive way to examine this: simulate under plausible data-generating processes, fit candidate analyses, and quantify how estimates and uncertainty change across modelling choices. In that sense, simulation is not only a design tool but also a diagnostic for robustness and identifiability (see also simulation-based approaches to understanding and validating complex models in ecology; \citealt{direnzoPracticalGuideUnderstanding2023}).

More broadly, choosing an analysis and deciding what counts as evidence is unavoidably epistemic: it depends on background assumptions about how data are generated, what uncertainty means, and what kinds of claims are warranted in the face of error and limited information. These questions are actively studied in philosophy of science and the foundations of statistics, but they often remain implicit in everyday practice \citep{romeijnPhilosophyStatistics2014, gelmanPhilosophyPracticeBayesian2013}. Making the generative model explicit turns many hidden choices into inspectable assumptions. It also clarifies that (i) precise estimates can still be wrong when key mechanisms or error processes are misspecified \citep{boxScienceStatistics1976}, and (ii) methodological choices can legitimately reflect ``inductive risk''---the consequences of different kinds of error---which motivates being explicit about what uncertainty is acceptable for a given claim \citep{douglasInductiveRiskValues2000, steelEpistemicValuesArgument2010}. In ecology and evolution, this perspective aligns with growing calls to improve uncertainty consideration and propagation beyond narrow reliance on single metrics, and to adopt clearer standards for reporting uncertainty and the consequences of model structure \citep{simmondsRecommendationsQuantitativeUncertainty2024, leleHowShouldWe2020}.

These benefits become increasingly important as ecology and evolution incorporate larger datasets, more complex models, and AI-assisted pattern discovery. Modern tools can detect subtle regularities in high-dimensional data, but they do not remove the need to decide what to believe and what uncertainty is defensible. Simulation-based thinking provides a practical way to expose and communicate assumptions, to quantify the consequences of modelling choices, and to distinguish predictive success from mechanistic understanding. In this sense, the simulation loop is both a design tool and a reasoning tool: it helps researchers and students move from compelling stories toward quantitative expectations, transparent uncertainty, and conclusions that are proportional to what the data can support.

At the same time, simulation does not guarantee correct inference: its value is conditional on the assumptions explored. It cannot by itself resolve confounding in observational studies or substitute for careful causal design \citep{pearlSevenToolsCausal2019}. But it can make these limitations explicit---for example, by showing that even very large datasets can yield narrow uncertainty intervals for the \emph{wrong} quantity if key mechanisms or measurement processes are misspecified. This matters especially in AI-enabled workflows, where model complexity and opacity can make it difficult to understand how conclusions were produced, and where standards for uncertainty quantification and error propagation remain uneven across tools and subfields \citep{pichlerMachineLearningDeep2023, lucasTranslucentBoxInterpretable2020}. By encouraging explicit generative assumptions and transparent reporting of their consequences, the simulation loop offers a practical step toward more defensible inference in a future where pattern discovery will be easy, but deciding what to believe may be harder than ever.



